State the water and activity conventions before using a logarithm (@chemistry-water-activities: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../general-chemistry/aqueous/pH-activity-and-measurement.lingua#r_3HDBH538NRM4TYDQ5SJVM04NRE") { r_00D580212A8C46020A7A7FDF06
Water participates in proton transfer. Use H⁺ as a compact notation for the hydrated proton description; the explicit water equation is 2H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺+OH⁻. We do not model an isolated bare proton moving independently of solvation. At fixed temperature and under a stated dilute-water model, the concentration-form relation is h o=K_w,c, where h=[H⁺], o=[OH⁻], and K_w,c has squared-concentration units.

A thermodynamic activity a_i is dimensionless and tied to a standard-state convention. On a specified concentration basis write a_i=γ_i c_i/c°, with dimensionless activity coefficient γ_i and reference concentration c°. For the formal acid reaction HA ⇌ H⁺+A⁻ with the solvent convention made implicit, its equilibrium expression is K_a=a_H a_A/a_HA. Replacing each activity by c_i/c° gives h[A⁻]/[HA]=K_a c°=K_a,c. Treating the solvent activity as one and the species coefficients as one is an ideal dilute approximation, not a consequence of charge balance. With nonunit coefficients the concentration relation changes; a fitted conditional coefficient must retain its conditions.

Likewise the dimensionless water product under the adopted solvent convention is K_w≈K_w,c/(c°)² in the ideal concentration model. Its value is conditional on temperature and the chosen model. The notation K_w,c distinguishes a dimensional concentration product from a dimensionless thermodynamic constant. The shared [Quantities, Units and Symbols in Physical Chemistry — IUPAC Green Book](../general-chemistry/sources/IUPAC-Green-Book.lingua#r_5SR8CP04E580VSV69MYQNSZ73F) supplies precise quantity/activity conventions; the full thermodynamic derivation remains later preparation.

pH is defined through the adopted hydrogen-ion activity convention. In our ideal concentration calculation pH≈−log₁₀(h/c°), using [Construct a logarithm from accumulated reciprocal change](Construct-a-logarithm-from-accumulated-reciprocal-change.lingua#r_048F416B6E95AE137A181635B7): log₁₀u=ln(u)/ln(10), with u>0. The inverse relation is h=c°10^(−pH). Every logarithm has a dimensionless argument. Changing the numerical unit from mol/L to mol/m³ multiplies both h and the numerical c° by 1000 and leaves their ratio unchanged.

In the neutral ideal water case h=o=√K_w,c, so pH_neutral=−(1/2)log₁₀(K_w,c/(c°)²). It is not universally seven. If a hypothetical model stipulates K_w,c=10⁻¹⁴ (mol/L)² and c°=1 mol/L, its neutral value is seven. The value is a teaching input here, not a new measurement or a claim that all temperatures and matrices share it.

A concentration-model prediction and an operational pH measurement are different evidence. Single-ion activities cannot be independently obtained without conventions, and an actual result requires a measurement procedure and calibration. Reuse [Connect pH with activity, the water model, and a calibrated observation](../general-chemistry/aqueous/pH-activity-and-measurement.lingua#r_3HDBH538NRM4TYDQ5SJVM04NRE) and its existing metrology references; do not call extra calculator digits a validated pH uncertainty.

**Read and work:** [Quantities, Units and Symbols in Physical Chemistry — IUPAC Green Book](../general-chemistry/sources/IUPAC-Green-Book.lingua#r_5SR8CP04E580VSV69MYQNSZ73F), the activity/equilibrium and electrochemistry quantity tables, and [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../general-chemistry/sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), section 14.2 with its concentration approximation made explicit. Convert h=2×10⁻⁷ mol/L into the exact expression 7−log₁₀2 under the hypothetical reference convention; recover h by inversion. Explain what changing K_w,c does to neutrality and what changing γ_H would do to the relation between h and activity. Do not assign an unmeasured activity coefficient merely to force agreement.

**Continue:** [Connect pH with activity, the water model, and a calibrated observation](../general-chemistry/aqueous/pH-activity-and-measurement.lingua#r_3HDBH538NRM4TYDQ5SJVM04NRE).

